A||||5||Linx||intake manifold gasketing||||4||
Z||000000||Linx||04-21-2000||05:43 PM||||Fellas-<BR>Im on the tail end of my 2.8 rebuild.  I'm reinstalling the intake manifold and I notice that the manifold isnt meeting with the block!<P>   <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/linxramblr/gasket.jpg">   <P><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://members.aol.com/linxramblr/gasket2.jpg">   <BR>I know I'm supposed to apply a bead of RTV at these points, but after I've torqed down the manifold, the gap is still huge.  The bead of RTV didnt even compress!<P>Sure, I guess I <I>could</I> use a fatter bead of RTV, but last time I did this- it blew out and oil went everywhere.<P>Anyone installed an intake manifold, and experience this big ol' gap?  didja just use lots of RTV?  What'd you do?<P><p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 04-21-2000).]||||reg||4
Z||000001||theogre||04-21-2000||06:29 PM||||V6 intake sets don't come with gaskets for that location? (Never bought a set for this motor.....)<P>Was that block or the heads milled? if so then the intake may also require milling to properly align. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Linx||04-21-2000||08:18 PM||||Nope. No milling done.  I recall there was RTV there when I first disassembled it- dont remember it being a thick bead tho'.<P>Book says use RTV.  I just cant imagine using that much RTV in an application.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Linx||04-21-2000||08:19 PM||||Holy cow.<P>I did some pokin' around, conferred with a few fellow shade tree mechanics and came up with the following- the huge gap between the block and intake manifold is <I>supposed</I> to be there.  If the heads were milled, <I>the gap would get narrower.</I>  If there were no gap- the moment you'd remilled your head surfaces...the manifold wouldnt fit!<P>And this is one of those rare occasions when you <I>do</I> use a fat bead of RTV.<p>[This message has been edited by Linx (edited 04-24-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Linx||04-24-2000||01:40 AM||||by the way- works perfectly.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Frank R||04-24-2000||11:45 AM||||In my history as line mechanic, method used to keep any gasket in position was to make  several lines of sharp punch marks.  The prick punch marks provide a small amout of resistance to any gasket or RTV from squeezing out.  Leave the surface rough.  Before applying sealants all surfaces must be dry and free from any other sealer or lubricant.  A quick wipe down with lacquer thinner is real good for a clean surface.<BR>Frank R||||reg||6
